ected yeah that was part of the  Act as well one of the anecdotes in the  book is that a new employee met with her  in 2011 and it was at the end of a long  day  she was getting up from her chair and  expressed excitement that he had joined  the company and she forgot to put on the  baritone for a moment and slipped into  her more natural sounding young woman's  voice and at that point it dawned on him  that that [ __ ] was a put-on  yeah it's so funny cuz everybody who  watched the 60 minutes piece again said  oh my gosh her voice is so unusual this  it's so deep but I wonder if that was  part of her way in a male-dominated  Silicon Valley of trying to and also in  the Investor world of trying to you know  portray sort of importance or whatever  was I mean there was this whole kind of  Mythology you've used that word cult  around her and I keep thinking - you  know we've had Steve Jobs we've had Mark  Zuckerberg we've had Bill Gates all men  who dropped out of Ivy League schools  like she did and people were rooting for  a woman to succeed in Silicon Valley  right we're rooting for Elizabeth Holmes  right there was a yearning for a  billionaire female startup founder I  mean there'd been other women in Silicon  Valley in recent years who risen to fame  like Marissa Mayer Sheryl Sandberg at  Facebook but they hadn't created the  companies they were the the hired help  yeah in a way and Elizabeth Holmes was  going to be the first female billionaire  founder and and people really wanted her  to succeed for that reason in part and  then what you say about Silicon Valley  is very true it's it's really  overwhelmingly a man's world and I think  she put on the voice the deeper voice to  be taken seriously and in a way I can  understand that so we talked about this  you know finger prick right where you  could get a couple drops drops of blood  the proprietary technology was that she  had developed was essentially the Edison  there was later a mini lab there at  Ford's of 